WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.252
This query combines two cleaning steps.

00:00:02.252 --> 00:00:08.677
BTRIM removes boundary spaces. REGEXP_REPLACE then collapses repeated internal whitespace to one space.

00:00:08.677 --> 00:00:15.732
The g flag means replace every matching occurrence, not just the first.

00:00:15.732 --> 00:00:19.982
Run the query and compare the raw and normalized names row by row.

00:00:19.982 --> 00:00:24.172
Connect this idea to an operations team aligning timestamps across locations.

00:00:24.172 --> 00:00:30.562
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:30.562 --> 00:00:37.527
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:37.527 --> 00:00:44.787
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:44.787 --> 00:00:49.600
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
